WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.120
AI? Whoa! Hybrid cloud? Whoa?

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.560
Wait a minute. What about mainframe?

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:06.840
What...?

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.320
Mainframe careers, they are opportunity

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.040
for everyone to be involved&nbsp;
in absolutely critical work

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:19.520
and get involved in the industry
and really have a job for a long time.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.720
Just like I have, 36 years!

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:22.760
But, you know,&nbsp;

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:27.520
let me introduce my co-speaker&nbsp;
today, Christina LaRow,

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:30.480
who is a new IBMer, to explain why

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.400
she chose to come into a mainframe career.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:39.080
So about eight months ago
when I joined IBM and prior to that,

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.920
I graduated college--
and I sent out job applications everywhere.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:44.640
We all know how that goes.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:46.720
And I wasn't hearing from anything.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:48.080
And then I was like with my best friend

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.600
one day who happened to have an internship at IBM
and then got a full time job offer.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.280
And he said, “Well, why don't&nbsp;
I forward your resume along?

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.600
See what happens.”
I said, “Okay, sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.600
why not?” Sent him the resume.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:00.920
And then a few weeks later&nbsp;

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:04.680
I got an email from Rosalind&nbsp;
saying, “Hey, why don't we chat?

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:08.440
I'll send you some contacts.”&nbsp;
Yeah, and you got to talk to

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:12.160
a number of people on the team
about what they were doing, right?

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:13.800
Yeah. And it was fascinating.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.960
Before that, I didn't really&nbsp;
know what the mainframe was&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:18.520
besides this ancient technology

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:20.240
that my parents use when they were kids.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.320
Or it was something that everyone was

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:26.920
kind of questioning me on
about why I was even entertaining this job offer.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.920
But what made you finally&nbsp;
decide to actually take the job?

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:33.520
Well, when we were talking with your contacts,
people like Frank and Jerry.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.400
they were so excited about the technology
and they were explaining

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.960
how impactful it is on society-- how

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.600
like on the economy and government,
just private organizations.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.720
So many people use mainframe.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:51.480
And that got me excited to work on a technology
that's impactful. And

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:55.720
looking at Jerry and Frank--&nbsp;
they're on the older side

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:59.400
and they kind of represented
where I thought the mainframe developer was.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:04.960
So I saw a lot of opportunity for myself
to grow in this role.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.120
You mean we're old and we're going to retire?

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.760
And so you can take over for all those jobs?

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:11.480
Your words, not mine!

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:16.800
Yeah. But I understand that&nbsp;
we do need a variety of ages

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.880
as part of the industry to&nbsp;
help make sure it grows.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.480
Okay, so now you're here.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:26.320
How did it-- how did you work out with that?

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.240
What did it turn out like?

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:29.760
Easier than I thought it would be.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:34.000
A lot of stuff I learned in&nbsp;
college applied to the mainframe,&nbsp;

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.360
which I was not expecting at all.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:38.800
I thought I'd have to learn&nbsp;
this really old archaic language

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:43.000
or work on like those old black and green screens.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.880
But no, I can use VS code.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:48.480
I use Python almost exclusively,

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.160
the [Eclipse] z/OS Explorer, which I had exposure to in college.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:53.280
I'm able to use that.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:56.360
The hardest part is all the Z terminology,

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:58.320
which I can just ask my teammates&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.880
or I can ask you or anyone&nbsp;
else within the vicinity.

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.440
It's kind of unique to sit next to you&nbsp;

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:07.360
so you can answer those&nbsp;
questions, and it's kind of fun.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:08.640
It's also good for me&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.760
because I get to learn more&nbsp;
about how you see the environment

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:18.200
and you see the mainframe and&nbsp;
how you're working with it.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.560
Now let's talk to everybody else.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.440
How would you suggest they get&nbsp;
started in a mainframe career?

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.360
Be open to it.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:28.360
People are going to come to you&nbsp;

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:31.040
and they're going to say, “Why&nbsp;
on earth are you doing this?

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:33.920
No one will be using it in ten years.”

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:35.360
Don't take their word at face value.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.080
Do your own research.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:41.640
Find out for yourself how&nbsp;
important this technology is.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:43.360
Second, when you do decide

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:45.280
that you want to pursue
this career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.680
don't be intimidated by all the acronyms.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:49.280
There are so many acronyms.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.000
You can Google that--it's fine.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.880
You can ask people--it's fine. Ask questions.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:56.520
Don't be afraid to go to&nbsp;
your teammates or anyone else&nbsp;

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:57.680
if you're in the office.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.760
Don't be afraid to look around you and say, “Hey,&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.000
what does ISPF mean?” Or any&nbsp;
other acronym or any other

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.000
just vocab word that you&nbsp;
don't know because no one here&nbsp;

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.920
expects you to know everything&nbsp;
about Z right off the bat.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.400
And that was a huge comfort for me going in.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:12.600
Ask question!.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:16.640
And if you are one of the people
that someone is asking a question to--be patient.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.640
They may ask dumb questions,&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.640
apparently no question’s a&nbsp;
dumb question and be patient.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:25.640
They're going to have follow up questions
and they're just trying to learn.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.200
So be excited to learn.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:28.960
Be open to questions.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.160
Everything will be fine.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:33.440
And that's really important&nbsp;
when we think about this.

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:35.160
No matter what job you take,&nbsp;

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.600
you need to be a continuous&nbsp;
learner for your lifetime.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:40.520
I learn new things every day.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:44.680
Talking to newer people&nbsp;
who've come into the industry

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:47.080
as well as technology continues to change.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:50.720
But the mainframe is a critical part of our world.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:52.680
It runs the economy.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:55.960
And it's time to take a&nbsp;
look at the mainframe today&nbsp;

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:59.640
and see the opportunities that are there.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:00.520
Thanks for watching.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:08.760
Before you leave, please remember&nbsp;
to hit like and subscribe.

